occupationalSummary:
&lt;p&gt;This occupation is found in the electrical transmission and distribution network industry. This industry is made up of employers of all sizes, such as network operators and their contractors. These organisations are responsible for the transmission and distribution of electricity from where it is generated, across the country through networks of overhead lines, underground cables and substations to where it is used by commercial and non-commercial customers. Power industry electrical systems engineers may work on the high voltage transmission network or the high or low voltage distribution network. This is a core and options apprenticeship standard. An apprentice must be trained and assessed on the core and one option relevant to their role. The options are: • electrical project engineer • operational delivery engineer • asset management engineer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The broad purpose of the occupation is to manage power system assets or work such as network alterations, installation of new plant and equipment and asset refurbishment work. Once the work is complete, Power industry electrical systems engineers carry out audits to check that work has been completed to safety and quality standards. Infrastructure projects of this nature are essential for the delivery of the Department for Energy Security and Net Zero strategic objectives, allowing the integration of green technologies such as wind and solar equipment into the network and supporting the connection of flexible energy upgrades, such as hydrogen, thermal generation, new nuclear and battery storage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Electrical project engineers ensure that projects are completed to time, budget and specification. They ensure that health, safety and environmental concerns are managed effectively throughout the project and key stakeholders are kept informed as work progresses.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Operational delivery engineers issue safety documents and work instructions to Working Parties, overseeing work where necessary to ensure it is carried out safely and to specification.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Asset management engineers monitor asset condition and performance, and use this to develop maintenance, inspection and refurbishment plans. When an asset fails, they contribute to the investigation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The electrical transmission and distribution industry operates 365 a year, so Power industry electrical systems engineers often work shifts or work an on-call rota. They typically work outdoors in all weathers and may require a driving licence to enable them to travel to work sites and infrastructure projects in their area. They will also be required to gain authorisations from their employer to enable them to complete certain tasks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In their daily work, an employee in this occupation interacts with the craftspersons, engineers and contractors that make up the Working Party or project team. They may also work with control room engineers to schedule works and communicate with customers to update on progress. A Power industry electrical systems engineer usually reports to a senior engineer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An employee in this occupation will be responsible for contributing to site and project safety by ensuring work is completed in line with the electricity system safety rules.&lt;/p&gt;
